could call it from an inspection path expecting a pure calculation, reuse the mutated options elsewhere, or remove the cleanup plugin without realizing it owns output deletion.\n\nRefactor it into explicit phases:\n\n- `resolveDtsRollupInputs(...)`: package/dependency reads and normalization.\n- `createDtsRollupConfig(...)`: pure config construction from resolved inputs.\n- Move `setSilent` into `startRollup`, where orchestration side effects belong.\n- Clone `options.dts` instead of mutating it.\n\n`buildStart` itself must not be renamed: it is a Rollup lifecycle hook. Extract its implementation instead:\n\n```ts\nconst cleanDeclarationOutput = async (): Promise<void> => { /* ... */ }\n\nconst cleanupPlugin: Plugin = {\n  name: \"tsup:clean\",\n  buildStart: cleanDeclarationOutput,\n}\n```\n\nThis preserves Rollup’s required property name while giving the destructive operation an honest name.\n\n### [`src/index.ts`](</private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/d0b4a6f6-acd0-4986-8afe-011e15f736b2/scratchpad/evalwt/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/index.ts:167>)\n\n`build` is a public API, so renaming it would be a breaking change. Keep it as a compatibility facade with an explicit return type:\n\n```ts\nexport async function build(options: Options): Promise<void> {\n  await runBuildPipeline(options)\n}\n```\n\nInternally, extract and name the effects:\n\n- `loadAndResolveBuildConfigs`\n- `runDeclarationBuild`\n- `runJavaScriptBuild`\n- `runBuildCycle`\n- `cleanBuildOutput`\n- `bundleAllFormats`\n- `copyPublicAssets`\n- `runSuccessHook`\n- `startBuildWatcher`\n\nRename `buildAll` to `runBuildCycle`. Today it does far more than build: it kills the previous success process, resets dependency state, cleans output, bundles every format, copies public files, and starts the success hook. An agent adding another format could accidentally perform global cleanup or success hooks once per format, or disturb their required ordering.\n\n## 2. `option_bag_junk_drawer`\n\nThe detector’s “implicit shape” wording is slightly overstated: `getRollupConfig` accepts the explicit `NormalizedOptions` type. The real problem is ownership—this declaration-only worker receives the entire system-wide option model while reading ten fields.\n\nIntroduce a narrow, immutable boundary such as:\n\n```ts\ntype DtsRollupRequest = Readonly<{\n  input: InputOption\n  compilerOptions: Record<string, unknown>\n  resolve: DtsConfig[\"resolve\"]\n  outputDirectory: string\n  outputs: readonly DtsOutputTarget[]\n  external: readonly (string | RegExp)[]\n  tsconfigPath?: string\n  banner?: string\n  footer?: string\n  shouldCleanDeclarations: boolean\n}>\n```\n\n`DtsOutputTarget` should contain the already-resolved format, declaration extension, and CJS interop decision. That avoids passing the full `NormalizedOptions` merely because `outExtension` needs it while extensions are being calculated.\n\nNormalize once at the worker boundary:\n\n- `clean` becomes `shouldCleanDeclarations`, preventing an agent from assuming custom clean patterns are honored here.\n- `dts.entry ?? entry` becomes one required `input`.\n- Output extensions are calculated before config construction.\n- Optional arrays become empty readonly arrays.\n- The returned request contains no reference to mutable `options.dts`.\n\nThis makes new dependencies visible: extending declaration bundling requires deliberately adding a field instead of silently reaching into the global bag.\n\n## 3. `return_shape_roulette`\n\n### [`src/esbuild/svelte.ts`](</private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/d0b4a6f6-acd0-4986-8afe-011e15f736b2/scratchpad/evalwt/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9>)\n\nThese three findings are largely one nested lexical cluster, not three public functions independently returning six shapes. The detector sees returns belonging to several protocols:\n\n- `onResolve`: `{ path, namespace }`\n- `onLoad`: `{ contents, warnings }` or `{ errors }`\n- esbuild messages: `{ text, location }`\n- Svelte preprocessing: `{ code }` or `{ code, map }`\n\nAn agent could put `code` where esbuild expects `contents`, return a diagnostic object directly from `onLoad`, or add a branch that TypeScript infers too broadly.\n\nExtract and annotate each protocol boundary:\n\n- `sveltePlugin(options): Plugin`\n- `setupSveltePlugin(build, options): void`\n- `resolveSvelteStyles: OnResolveCallback`\n- `createSvelteLoader(...): OnLoadCallback`\n- `loadSvelteComponent(...): Promise<OnLoadResult>`\n- `toEsbuildMessage(...): PartialMessage`\n- A typed Svelte `script` preprocessor returning its documented processed shape.\n\nFor the internal compilation step, use a discriminated result:\n\n```ts\ntype SvelteCompileResult =\n  | { ok: true; contents: string; warnings: PartialMessage[] }\n  | { ok: false; errors: PartialMessage[] }\n```\n\nConvert that result to esbuild’s `OnLoadResult` in one adapter. Do not add a custom discriminator to esbuild’s external return object.\n\n## Delivery order and verification\n\n1. Refactor the Svelte callbacks first; it is the most isolated module.\n2. Split and narrow the Rollup configuration path.\n3. Refactor `src/index.ts` last, preserving the exported `build` facade because it is the broadest and most load-bearing surface.\n4. Add characterization tests for Svelte error/warning shapes, declaration cleanup and extension selection, build-cycle ordering, watch rebuilds, and success-hook cleanup.\n5. Run the package build and tests, then repeat the JSON scan. The target is removal of these eight findings without changing output artifacts, watch semantics, or the public `build(options): Promise<void>` contract.\n\nNo files were changed.",
